We can simplify the expression by canceling out common factors in the numerator and denominator:
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)\left(\frac{3}{4}\right)\left(\frac{4}{5}\right)\left(\frac{5}{6}\right) &= \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{3}{4} \cdot \frac{4}{5} \cdot \frac{5}{6} \\
&= \frac{2 \cdot 3 \cdot 4 \cdot 5}{3 \cdot 4 \cdot 5 \cdot 6} \\
&= \frac{2 \cdot \cancel{3} \cdot \cancel{4} \cdot \cancel{5}}{\cancel{3} \cdot \cancel{4} \cdot \cancel{5} \cdot 6} \\
&= \frac{2}{6} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{1}{3}}.
\end{align*}
The answer is: \frac{1}{3}

